# The editor-side project repository

Editing a literate program creates a problem that a command-line generator does not
have. The text an author sees may not yet exist on disk. Several workspace folders
may be open at once. A file can change again while an earlier request is still
reading its neighbors. Completion needs a quick, read-only answer, while an explicit
processing request may eventually replace generated files.

The editor therefore needs more than a parser. It needs a boundary that captures a
coherent project, presents that project to the pure engine, and publishes the result
to editor features without turning every question into a filesystem side effect.
That boundary is the editor-side project repository.

## A repository is a view, not a second language implementation

The word *repository* can suggest a database that owns the truth. Here it means
something narrower: an adapter-maintained view of each workspace folder. The
literate documents and templates remain the inputs, and the headless engine remains
the authority on their meaning. The repository coordinates editor state around that
authority.

For each workspace folder it retains the most recently accepted source snapshot,
fragment occurrences, parser locations used by navigation, the effective preview
theme, and the diagnostics owned by Literate. These values are related, so they are
replaced as one processing result rather than updated independently.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Folder[One workspace folder] --> Capture[Capture current project text]
    Capture --> Engine[Interpret with the shared engine]
    Engine --> Diagnostics[Generation diagnostics]
    Engine --> Inspection[Accepted fragments and locations]
    Inspection --> Symbols[Editor symbol snapshot]
    Inspection --> Preview[Preview model and theme]
    Engine --> Plan[Optional output plan]
    Plan --> Gate{Were writes requested and is the plan valid?}
    Gate -->|No| Stop[Keep the operation read-only]
    Gate -->|Yes| Transaction[Preflight and commit changed outputs]
```

The arrows show the flow of one captured interpretation. They do not mean that
editor features call one another. Diagnostics, symbols, preview, and an optional
write plan are sibling consequences of the same input. The diagram intentionally
omits the details of expansion and output transactions, which belong to the engine
and filesystem chapters.

In the implementation this coordinating adapter is `FragmentRepository`. Its name
reflects the editor feature that first consumes it, but its architectural role is
broader: it owns the lifetime of editor-side project snapshots, not fragment
semantics.

## Capture what the author can currently see

A snapshot begins with project membership. Literate discovers every `.literate`
document in a workspace folder, including chapters that are not linked from the
root index. Directories used for dependencies, version-control data, generated
builds, and test runtimes are excluded by the same policy used by the command-line
adapter. This keeps host differences from changing which project the engine sees.

Discovery and reading answer different questions. Discovery asks which paths
belong to the project. Reading asks which text currently represents each path. If a
discovered path has an open editor buffer, that buffer wins over disk—even when it
is empty. Otherwise the adapter reads the saved file. New file-backed buffers can
join the snapshot before their first save; untitled documents cannot, because they
have no stable workspace-relative identity.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Discover[Discover eligible project paths] --> Path[Consider one canonical path]
    Open{Is that exact URI open?}
    Path --> Open
    Open -->|Yes| Buffer[Capture the complete buffer text]
    Open -->|No| Disk[Read the saved file]
    Buffer --> Snapshot[Add one path and one text]
    Disk --> Snapshot
    Snapshot --> Order[Apply semantic document order]
```

The important detail is the decision by presence, not by nonempty content. An empty
buffer means the author removed everything; falling back to disk would silently
revive text that is no longer visible. URI identity also matters in a multi-root
workspace, where equal relative names can belong to different projects.

After source capture, the engine identifies template dependencies. The adapter then
captures those templates with the same open-buffer precedence. A missing template
stays absent so the engine can report the error at the declaration that requested
it. Other read failures abort the operation rather than constructing a misleading
partial snapshot.

Finally, the engine's ordering policy puts the root document first, follows linked
chapters in narrative order, and then includes remaining sources deterministically.
Filesystem enumeration order never becomes fragment contribution order by accident.

## One queue gives changing inputs a comprehensible order

Editor events can overlap. A keystroke may trigger processing while a completion
request asks for a refresh, or publication may begin while generation is still
installing files. Allowing these operations to mutate shared state concurrently
would make their outcome depend on timing.

The repository therefore serializes work. Each request waits for earlier work to
finish, captures its own inputs, asks the engine for a result, and only then publishes
its replacement state. A failure is reported but does not poison the queue; later
requests can still run.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Event as Editor event or command
    participant Repo as Project repository
    participant Store as Buffers and workspace storage
    participant Engine as Pure engine

    Event->>Repo: Request refresh with write intent
    Note over Repo: Wait for earlier repository work
    Repo->>Store: Discover and capture current inputs
    Store-->>Repo: Documents and templates
    Repo->>Engine: Interpret explicit snapshot
    Engine-->>Repo: Diagnostics, inspection, publication model, plan
    Repo-->>Event: Publish coherent editor state
    opt Writes were explicitly requested and the plan is valid
        Repo->>Store: Preflight, stage, and commit changed outputs
    end
```

The `write intent` carried by a request is as important as its trigger. Providers
such as completion, definition, references, hover, rename preparation, and code
actions request a read-only refresh. Asking a question must not generate source or
HTML. Explicit processing, literate document changes, recognized input-file events,
and newly added workspace folders may request generation with writes enabled.
Publication joins the same queue but uses its own fresh capture and isolated helper
pipeline.

The queue orders effects; it does not freeze the editor. If the author changes a
buffer while a provider is awaiting refresh, that provider must compare versions or
capture again before proposing an edit. Freshness remains the responsibility of the
caller that will expose or apply the result.

## Scheduling follows causes, not just files

Open-buffer changes and saved-file changes arrive through different host channels.
A text-document event already identifies the changed buffer and can request work
directly. Filesystem notifications often arrive in bursts, so they are briefly
coalesced per workspace folder. A burst in one root does not delay an unrelated
root, although all repository operations still enter the common serial queue.

The input filter recognizes literate documents and templates currently named by the
project. It ignores known generated destinations, even if one happens to end in
`.literate`, so generation cannot trigger itself indefinitely. Removing a workspace
folder cancels its pending timer and discards every cache keyed by that folder's full
URI. Adding a folder captures an initial view.

This scheduling is deliberately conservative. A filesystem change to a template
currently named by the project is recognized and coalesced like other input changes.
Inputs that have not yet become project dependencies cannot grant themselves that
authority. Obsolete generated outputs are not inferred and deleted, avoiding a
destructive conclusion from an incomplete event stream.

## Publish diagnostics and navigation from the same result

A malformed header, missing family, cycle, invalid path, or absent template is a
semantic problem. The engine reports it once. The editor adapter translates its
workspace-relative location into an editor range and replaces Literate's diagnostics
for that workspace. It does not ask navigation helpers to invent a second validation
policy.

Replacement matters when a project is repaired: a successful result may contain no
errors, so the previous entries must be removed. Workspace scoping matters too: a
refresh in one root must not clear problems in another. VS Code calls the resulting
container a `DiagnosticCollection`; the concept is simply ownership of one named set
of Problems entries.

Navigation state is rebuilt even when generation cannot produce files. Definitions,
mentions, and valid fragment contributions remain useful while the project is
incomplete. The repository first installs empty containers, then fills them from the
new ordered snapshot. If ordering itself fails, no earlier valid map survives to
misdirect a later request.

The engine's inspection identifies accepted contributions. A Markdown parser then
adds host-facing token locations for existing editor commands. This second parse
does not expand fragments, render the book, or decide which declarations are valid.
It attaches editor coordinates to decisions already made by shared semantics.

## Preview reads a snapshot without joining the effect queue

Markdown preview rendering is synchronous from the parser's point of view. It cannot
wait for discovery or filesystem reads. The repository therefore supplies the last
discovered project as a base, overlays every currently open literate buffer, and
finally overlays the exact source being rendered. Parsing this in-memory view is
read-only.

This arrangement has a useful division of responsibility:

- queued processing discovers membership, captures templates, owns diagnostics, and
  may commit outputs when asked;
- synchronous preview overlays visible text on the cached membership and creates no
  files;
- language services capture a fresh snapshot independently and reject it if inputs
  change during asynchronous acquisition.

These are different freshness contracts around the same pure semantic core. Treating
them as one cache would either block preview on I/O or let generation rely on a
partial display snapshot.

## The boundary makes future editor features cheaper

Once the repository can provide a coherent, read-only project view, editor features
become projections rather than miniature parsers. They can ask which symbol occupies
a position, where its family begins, which occurrences share its identity, or which
semantic target belongs in a preview link. New features should continue this pattern:
request explicit state, remain read-only until the user chooses an edit, and let the
engine own language meaning.

That leads to the next question. A snapshot knows the whole project, but how should
an editor turn fragment names scattered through prose and code into symbols that can
be completed, navigated, inspected, and changed safely?
